{"text": "2048 Friendly Place\nCrofton, MD 21114\ndune 21, 1986\n\nPresident Pieter W. Botha\nSouth African Embassy\n3051 Massachusetts Ave., N.W.\nWashington, D.C. 20008\n\nDear President Botha:\n\nI am writing to let you know that I think the time is well overdue for\nyou to be responsible to all your countrymen. You must understand and realize\nthat you should not have a superiority complex over your countrymen based\non their skin color which happens to be black.\n\nI would Tike you to stop ordering the murder and the slaughter of the\nblack South Africans because they simply want to enjoy the same freedom and\nliberties as the white South Africans and you possess. You should not have\nthem killed because you are white and have the power to do so.\n\nGod made provisions for all of us. We breathe the same air, have means\nfor food and a earth to live on. He made people of different colors. I do\nnot know why, but we all have a few things in common. We are human beings;\nwe have the same desires; and we must all die. I am quite sure every person\nwould want to die a natural death rather than be killed or murdered by an\ninsensitive government.\n\nI do not understand why, because you happen to be a white person that\nyou deny your black countrymen\u2019 the right to life on earth. We are people\ntoo.\n\nI would like to know what you would do if God had made your skin color\nblack. Would you want to be treated in the fashion that you have treated\nothers? How would you feel if your government did everything it could to\ndeny you your natural rights, order you killed and took your life at an early\nage because you expressed dissatisfaction with the treatment you received.\n\nMy only wish is for your skin to turn black. If this happens, then the\napartheid system in your country will cause you to lose your power, wealth,\nand dignity, force you to live in a matchbox in the squatter camp in Soweto,\nbe subjected to torture and inhumane treatment. You shall then understand\nthe true feelings of being a black South African. I wonder if you would have\nbeen one of those who would survive the Soweto riots in 1976 or whether you\nwould have been one of those young: children killed by the security forces.\n\nMr. Botha, I do not know you personally, only through what I have read\nin the papers and have seen on TV about the turmoil in South Africa. [, however,\nsincerely urge you to do yourself and the country a favor, put a little more\nlove in your heart, treat all your countrymen alike, make your country a\nbetter place to live for all people. Remember, we are all here on earth for\nonly a little while, make it as pleasant as you can for everyone. Do not\nmake it miserable for others just becaus\u00a2of their skin color.",
